Hello, I am working on understanding the lavaan syntax that is automatically generated when using the measEq.syntax() function from semTools. What I can't figure out is why does the syntax appear to place each item twice?

Using the supplied example,

mod.cat <http://mod.cat> <- ' FU1 =~ u1 + u2 + u3 + u4
                    FU2 =~ u5 + u6 + u7 + u8 '
## the 2 factors are actually the same factor (FU) measured twice
longFacNames <- list(FU = c("FU1","FU2"))

## configural model: no constraints across groups or repeated measures
syntax.config <- measEq.syntax(configural.model = mod.cat <http://mod.cat>, data = datCat,
                               ordered = paste0("u", 1:8),
                               parameterization = "theta",
                               ID.fac = "std.lv <http://std.lv>", ID.cat = "Wu.Estabrook.2016",
                               group = "g", longFacNames = longFacNames)
## print lavaan syntax to the Console
cat(as.character(syntax.config))

The first part of the generated syntax is:

## LOADINGS:

FU1 =~ c(NA, NA)*u1 + c(lambda.1_1.g1, lambda.1_1.g2)*u1
FU1 =~ c(NA, NA)*u2 + c(lambda.2_1.g1, lambda.2_1.g2)*u2
FU1 =~ c(NA, NA)*u3 + c(lambda.3_1.g1, lambda.3_1.g2)*u3
FU1 =~ c(NA, NA)*u4 + c(lambda.4_1.g1, lambda.4_1.g2)*u4
...
.

Why is the factor FU1 measured by the same item u1 twice and why are the labels between the two groups only supplied in the second instance of each item? Would the following syntax be equivalent?

FU1 =~ c(lambda.1_1.g1, lambda.1_1.g2)*u1 + c(lambda.2_1.g1, lambda.2_1.g2)*u2  + c(lambda.3_1.g1, lambda.3_1.g2)*u3 + c(lambda.4_1.g1, lambda.4_1.g2)*u4
...

Thanks for any help on this point!

- Noah

